     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mul.m2"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mulx[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 3: normalize. 0.05 has a leading zero, so shift left and subtract 1: 0.5 times 10 to the 4."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mul.m3"
      },
      {
       "action": "reveal",
       "target": "mulx[2]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mulx[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 4: round to the available digits. Rounding can carry all the way up, so it may need one more normalizing shift."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mul.m4"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Edge case with the bias: each stored exponent already has 127 added. So add them, and subtract 127 once. 130 plus 129 minus 127 is 132, meaning 2 to the 5."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "reveal",
       "target": "mulx[4]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mulx[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "mulx[4]",
       "delay": 0.4
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The mantissa multiply is the slow step, so it is split up itself: a tree of carry-save adders sums the partial products over several stages. Real FP multipliers have around 4 to 7 stages."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "mul.m2"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
